  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure passing to our south along a nearly stationary \nfrontal boundary will continue to bring a period of light \nrainfall through the first half of tonight. No flooding issues \nare expected with rainfall amounts generally ranging from a \ntenth of an inch or less from Augusta and points north and east \nwith a quarter to a half inch elsewhere. Fog is likely overnight\nas light winds allow the low-level moisture to stick around and\nit may become locally dense. Low temperatures will range from \nthe lower 40s across much of interior ME with middle to upper \n40s elsewhere. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nConfidence is high that fair weather will prevail Monday through at \nleast Wednesday with fair weather likely continuing into Friday. \nBroad high pressure over southern Canada will be the source of this \nfair weather as it drifts east through the week. As the high remains \ncentered north of the area, temperatures will trend cooler than \nnormal for both day time highs and overnight lows. This will \ntranslate to highs in the low to mid 60s and lows in the 30s to 40s. \nThere will likely be favorable radiational cooling conditions \nmost nights and it is likely some spots south of the mountains \nwill see some frost.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nFor the past several model cycles there has been a signal for a cut \noff upper low near the East Coast spawning at least one surface low \nin the vicinity of the Gulf Stream. While this signal has been \nsomewhat consistent there has been large model to model and run to \nrun variability in the strength and track of a potential coastal low \nnear the East Coast late in the week through next weekend. The \ncurrent envelope of solutions ranges from mostly a miss south and \neast of the area to a seasonably strong area of low pressure near \nthe Gulf of Maine. The former will still likely bring some high surf \nto the area and possibly some measurable rain, while the latter will \nbring greater potential for coastal impacts as well as gusty winds \nand rain into the interior. At this time, this system is around\n6 days out so it will likely take a couple more days to gain \nsome confidence in the track and strength of this potential \nsystem.",
